{"name":"Blue Grass","hex":"#007a7c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue grass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative spelling of bluegrass."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_grass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Poa","blocks":[{"text":"Poa is a genus of about 570 species of grasses, native to the temperate regions of both hemispheres. Common names include meadow-grass, bluegrass, tussock, and speargrass. Poa is Greek for 'fodder'. Poa are members of the subfamily Pooideae of the family Poaceae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Poa_annua.jpg/330px-Poa_annua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poa","wikidata":"Q157656"},{"title":"Poa pratensis","blocks":[{"text":"Poa pratensis, commonly known as Kentucky bluegrass, smooth meadow-grass, or common meadow-grass, is a perennial species of grass native to practically all of Europe, North Asia and the mountains of Algeria, Morocco, and Tunisia. There is disagreement about its native status in North America, with some sources considering it native and others stating the Spanish Empire brought the seeds of Kentucky bluegrass to the New World in mixtures with other grasses. It is a very popular lawn grass in North America with the species being spread over all of the cool, humid parts of the United States. In its native range, Poa pratensis forms a valuable pasture plant, characteristic of well-drained, fertile soil. It is also used for making lawns in parks and gardens and has established itself as a common invasive weed across cool moist temperate climates like the Pacific Northwest and the Northeastern United States. When found on native grasslands in Canada, for example, it is considered an unwelcome exotic plant, and is indicative of a disturbed and degraded landscape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Veldbeemdgras_Poa_pratensis.jpg/330px-Veldbeemdgras_Poa_pratensis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poa_pratensis","wikidata":"Q147783"},{"title":"Grass","blocks":[{"text":"Grass refers to various families of plants. The three major families of grasslike plants are true grasses (Poaceae), sedges (Cyperaceae), and rushes (Juncaceae). Lawns and pasturelands are typically composed of true grasses, five of which cover 46% of the world's arable land: rice, wheat, maize, barley, and sugar cane."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Poa_annua.jpg/330px-Poa_annua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grass","wikidata":"Q643352"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Bluegrass-stem.jpg/500px-Bluegrass-stem.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Bluegrass-stem.jpg/1280px-Bluegrass-stem.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Bluegrass-stem.jpg/500px-Bluegrass-stem.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Bluegrass\": The seed pods go from green to purplish blue to brown. During the purplish blue phase the seed stems have a navy-blue coating.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABluegrass-stem.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Ruwbeemdgras_Poa_trivialis_ligula.jpg/500px-Ruwbeemdgras_Poa_trivialis_ligula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Ruwbeemdgras_Poa_trivialis_ligula.jpg/500px-Ruwbeemdgras_Poa_trivialis_ligula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Poa trivialis (rough meadow-grass), showing the ligule structure","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARuwbeemdgras_Poa_trivialis_ligula.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/PP_Na_Popovickem_kopci_007_cf_Myrmus_miriformis.jpg/500px-PP_Na_Popovickem_kopci_007_cf_Myrmus_miriformis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/PP_Na_Popovickem_kopci_007_cf_Myrmus_miriformis.jpg/1280px-PP_Na_Popovickem_kopci_007_cf_Myrmus_miriformis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/PP_Na_Popovickem_kopci_007_cf_Myrmus_miriformis.jpg/500px-PP_Na_Popovickem_kopci_007_cf_Myrmus_miriformis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Myrmus miriformis in Přerov, Czech Republic","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APP_Na_Popovickem_kopci_007_cf_Myrmus_miriformis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi